{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:3535e056eef590d7f8b6917703a1133baad71b608cf5bb5dc29583aa57ba23f4","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.15}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"ANCHOR. G7 GDP-weighted (2025 PPP) 10y real today, weights summing to 1.000: US 0.515 x 2.60 = 1.339; Japan 0.117 x 0.40 = 0.047; Germany 0.105 x 0.50 = 0.053; UK 0.079 x 1.60 = 0.126; France 0.076 x 1.15 = 0.087; Italy 0.062 x 2.00 = 0.124; Canada 0.046 x 1.65 = 0.076. SUM = 1.852 -> anchor 1.85%.\nDECOMPOSITION OF THE 2032-2036 AVERAGE AS r* + REAL TERM PREMIUM - REPRESSION WEDGE (+ measurement).\nr* deltas vs today: life-cycle dissaving, old-age ratio 31 -> 40pp at ~+4bp of r* per pp in the dissaving phase net of accumulation-phase saving = +0.35; public dissaving, ~+20pp of debt/GDP at ~+2.5bp per 10pp through the r* channel (the rest runs through term premium) = +0.05; AI/grid capex demand for savings, +0.25 pre-bust but -0.20 post-bust at the locked 66% -> 0.34(+0.25) + 0.66(-0.20) = -0.05; lower g from the care/physical cap and the China contraction = -0.35; precautionary saving (China, aging, policy uncertainty) = -0.15; cognitive-labour compression shifting income to capital-concentrated, higher-propensity-to-save holders = -0.10. NET r* = -0.25pp.\nReal term premium: +0.55pp (gross public debt >100% of GDP by 2029, US ~142% and China ~127% by 2031, net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP, P95 3-yr Debt-at-Risk 117% and +2.4pp under a 20% AI equity shock, eroding sovereign convenience yields, marginal duration absorbed by levered basis trades that de-risk procyclically rather than by price-insensitive real money, DB plans turning from duration buyers to net payers).\nMeasurement: nominal-minus-breakeven embeds an inflation risk premium plus a TIPS liquidity premium, +0.15pp in exactly the high-uncertainty states being forecast; the 2030 RPI-to-CPIH alignment mechanically lifts the UK leg 0.6-1.0pp, worth +0.05-0.08pp on the aggregate.\nFinancial-repression wedge: P(explicit or soft repression binding inside the window) = 0.25 at -1.2pp when it binds -> expected -0.30pp.\nSUM: 1.85 - 0.25 + 0.55 + 0.15 - 0.30 = 2.00; I take P50 = 1.95 after netting the Japan leg's BoJ normalisation against the PPP-weighting lift to Italy.\nDO NOT MAP 2A's (r-g) ONTO THIS. The IMF's r is the effective interest rate on the outstanding stock, a backward-looking average coupon that lags the 10y marginal real yield by 100-150bp during a rate-rise phase. The locked +1.0pp (r-g) deterioration is consistent with my P50 only because most of the move is in g, not r.\nDISPERSION IS BIMODAL, NOT NOISE: repression and term-premium spiral are two opposite institutional answers to one fiscal identity. P10 0.90% (repression, or post-bust disinflation with flight-to-quality and core under 1.5%); P90 3.15% (no bust, Debt-at-Risk tail realises, AI capex keeps real investment demand high). Right-of-centre relative to any structural r* because of the two measurement biases above.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.690Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkish annual CPI, as published by TurkStat, averages below 7.0% over calendar 2027-2029 (three-year arithmetic mean of the December-on-December prints), with Mexico, Chile and Colombia each averaging below 3.5% over the same three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the EM-OECD floor that my CQ2 depends on: the GDP-weighted OECD-38 core aggregate falls from 2.556 to ~2.40, and P(core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036) drops from 0.52 to ~0.33. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~24. It also lowers the back-half core central from 2.30 to ~2.15, cutting CQ6-C from 15 to ~10 and raising D from 17 to ~22, with B roughly unchanged. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my numbers because it attacks the term I have made the largest and most mechanical.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI shelter (BLS series for shelter, December-on-December) averages below 2.4%/yr over calendar 2027-2030, while US medical care services plus the social-assistance component of PCE services averages below 2.6%/yr over the same four years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Breaks the two line items that carry 1.35pp of my 2.335 US core build. US core falls to ~1.95, the OECD-38 aggregate to ~2.40, and the front-loaded path I rely on (2.85 then 2.37) collapses to a flat ~2.2. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~22; CQ6-C from 15 to ~9 and D from 17 to ~24. It would also confirm the state-monopsony-rations-care channel over the care-prices-clear channel, which lowers CQ1's second conjunct further (from 0.12 to ~0.07) and so CQ1 from 8 to ~5.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Announced hyperscaler plus utility-scale firm-capacity additions serving data centres in the OECD-38 and China reach cumulative in-service (commercial-operation-date) capacity of at least 70 GW of net firm supply by end-2031, as reported in EIA/ENTSO-E/CEC interconnection and commissioning data, with cumulative publicly disclosed AI-related capex impairments and cancellations below 10% of announced buildouts.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my coupled claim that the physical trades and interconnection bind harder than the dollars. AI-Q4 resolves toward NO, so my 2032-2036 window is NOT the post-bust half: capital-services growth stays at ~1.8%/yr (+0.30pp on the OECD leg), the AI savings-demand term returns to +0.25pp on r*. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.65 to ~1.95 and P90 to ~2.9; CQ4 P50 from 1.95 to ~2.30; CQ6-A from 10 to ~17, D from 17 to ~11, B to ~57. CQ5's first conjunct falls (back-half MOVE nearer 85) taking CQ5 from 25 to ~20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS headline CPI prints a calendar-year average of 2.0% or higher in any two consecutive years during 2027-2031, with the non-food, non-residence component contributing at least 1.0pp of it (i.e. not a pork or energy spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills CQ2's stronger conjunct. Two years at 2.0%+ raise the 10-year average by ~0.2pp from my 1.05 central and, more importantly, signal that the household-income-share and property channels have reflated; P(China CPI < 1.50%) falls from 0.75 to ~0.40 and CQ2 from 36 to ~20. It would also raise China's nominal demand path, lifting my China real leg toward 3.3% and the combined CQ3 P50 to ~1.80, and shifting ~4pp of CQ6 mass from D into B and C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD harmonized unemployment (OECD-total, Short-Term Labour Market Statistics) averages 5.3% or below across calendar 2029-2031 while Eurostat SES 2030 and US BLS OES show ISIC F+Q median gross hourly earnings outpacing ISIC J+K+M by at least 4.5 percentage points cumulatively from 2022/2025 to 2030.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the only signpost that can rescue CQ1 and it attacks my composition arithmetic directly: a realized 4.5pp gap by 2030 implies the differential is running at ~0.8-0.9pp/yr rather than my +0.02pp/yr net, meaning either the entrant-dilution deduction (-0.40) or the J+K+M survivor-median deduction (-0.40) is wrong. Conjunct B's marginal rises from 0.12 to ~0.45 and conjunct A to ~0.60, taking CQ1 from 8 to ~32. It would also imply the care bottleneck is clearing through PRICES, which raises my back-half core central by ~0.2pp and moves CQ6-C from 15 to ~21.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 fermi seat, effort 'inflation'."}}